It is crucial to understand the implication in practise. Say, you wrote
a scroll, failed (the charges get lost and the scroll resource vanishes),
repeat that 3 more times and failed. You still cannot assume anything for
a subsequent try by inspecting your failed last tries. What makes things
worse is that (as opposed to, say, our example with the dungeon throwbacks,
where we had an average over ~22 levels) here we have a smaller set of
triggerable events (i.e. constrained by the few marker charges, which
provide, all averaged, just 4 scrolls of genocide) to sensibly rely on
averaging out with large sample sizes, so outliers may have a more
significant impact. Chances may look fine, nonetheless. So you have to
decide whether you need a guaranteed result or whether you don't care
about a guarantee and want to just maximise the outcome in case of success.

